The reference to another resource takes the form:
```
@[+][<package>:]<type>/<name>
```
The theme attribute is in the form:
```
?[<package>:]<type>/<name>
```
Your task is to write a function that takes a Java code snippet as input and extracts all the attribute names defined within the documentation comments.

Write a function with the following signature:
```java
public List<String> extractAttributeNames(String javaCodeSnippet)
```

The function should return a list of attribute names extracted from the Java code snippet. If no attribute names are found, the function should return an empty list.

For example, given the input:
```java
/**
 * This is a sample Java code snippet.
 * It contains attribute references like @[+][com.example]:type/name
 * and theme attributes like ?android:type/name.
 * @attr name com.example.musicplayer:logo
 */
```
The function should return the list:
```
[com.example:type/name, android:type/name, com.example.musicplayer:logo]
```

### Solution:
```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class AttributeExtractor {
    public List<String> extractAttributeNames(String javaCodeSnippet) {
        List<String> attributeNames = new ArrayList<>();
        Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile("(?<=@attr name )\\S+|(?<=\\*\\s)(@[+]?[\\w.]*:[\\w/]*)|(?<=\\*\\s)(\\?[\\w.]*:[\\w/]*)");
        Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(javaCodeSnippet);

        while (matcher.find()) {
            attributeNames.add(matcher.group());
        }

        return attributeNames;
    }

